# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .dockerignore is a security control, not a build-speed optimisation.
#
# Without it, `COPY . .` ships your .git directory into the image — including
# every secret anyone ever committed and later "removed", because removing a
# file in a later commit does not remove it from history. This is one of the
# most common ways credentials end up in published images.
#
# Deny by default, then allow what the build needs. An allowlist cannot leak
# a file you forgot to think about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Everything, unless allowed below.
**

# --- Application source the build actually needs ---------------------------
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!tsconfig.json
!src/
!public/

# --- Re-exclude things that slip back in under the allowed paths -----------
**/node_modules
**/*.test.ts
**/*.spec.ts
**/__tests__
**/__snapshots__

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# For reference, the deny-list style — easier to start with, easier to get
# wrong, and the reason images ship with credentials in them:
#
#   .git
#   .github
#   .env
#   .env.*
#   *.pem
#   *.key
#   .aws
#   .ssh
#   .npmrc
#   node_modules
#   Dockerfile*
#   docker-compose*.yml
#   .terraform
#   *.tfstate*
#   coverage
#   .vscode
#   .idea
#
# Verify whichever style you pick, rather than trusting it:
#   docker build -t app:test .
#   docker run --rm app:test ls -la          # if there is a shell
#   docker save app:test | tar -tv | grep -E '\.git|\.env|\.pem'
#
# On a distroless image there is no shell to check with, so inspect the
# exported filesystem instead. Do this once, when you write the file. It takes
# two minutes and it is the only thing that proves the ignore rules work.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
